veloping  there are some conflicting studies but  most of the studies that I've looked at  are in support of it they say that where  it's legal opioids are less likely to be  prescribed and I haven't looked into it  further but I asked that question I was  at a opioid seminar at the University of  Georgia recently there were two scholars  two researchers there and I was like  good bad there like mostly good again I  need to do more research on that myself  so maybe we'll step back a little bit  from the preventing addiction in the  first place and go a little more towards  dealing with addiction where where it  exists what do we know right now about  the different ways in which addiction  should be handled is most effectively  treated right so we know that people  respond to addiction addiction treatment  in different ways and so all all methods  of treatment should be offered I mean I  like to say that the treatment should be  as accessible as the street drugs are  but we know that's far from true only  one in ten people with substance abuse  disorder get any treatment at all and  only one in five who were addicted to  heroin or opioid pills get specialty  treatment so we're not even close to you  know reaching the number of people got  2.6 million Americans addicted right now  some people think it's as high as four  million and if we arrested every drug  dealer we would still have that many  people addicted so what didn't we do all  the evidence supports medication  assisted treatment and that is methadone  and buprenorphine in particular maybe  you've probably heard a methadone going  back to the 70s it was popular after  people came back from Vietnam addicted  but buprenorphine which goes by the  brand name suboxone has also been found  to be really effective and both of these  drugs these m-80s as I call them are  effective at reducing relapse and  overdose and criminal behavior and fifty  to sixty percent of cases as opposed to  abstinence only modalities which are  around ten some people say the last  percent of  now if you talk to people who've gone to  these out-of-state rehabs  abstinence-only and they're doing well  now they are absolutely converts if you  talk to people who have done m80 and  they're doing well their absolute  converts as well but they're less likely  to talk to you on the record because  there's such a stigma around m80  particularly coming from the 12-step  people so I think that's the number one  barrier to turning back this epidemic  the stigma against people who are on m80  many of those that I interviewed in my  book are now like getting their kids  back they're getting their lives  together going to school one woman's  becoming a nurse another was a kid who I  first met when he was just out of high  school and he had taken a job at a  senior living moving company with the  express purpose of stealing people's  pills that's how bad his addiction was  he was a doctor's son he's now getting a  full ride for a ph.d program I mean he's  doing great but it was MIT that allowed  him to get better from that and there's  just I mean there shouldn't be  controversy but because this country  were so entrenched in 12 steps and  abstinence-only and if you take another  drug for a drug addiction you're just  trying to replace one with another that  really holds us back so I just think  more people need to understand the  nuances medication assisted treatment  especially in the law enforcement and  drug court community what about more  thinking about this at the government  level I mean we heard from the current  president that Golden Boy Jared Kushner  I guess during breaks from solving the  israeli-palestinian conflict was going  to deal with the opioid crisis as well I  mean hey what can we say one way or the  other about what the Trump  administration's role has been so far in  dealing with this well president Trump  has said you know we should execute drug  dealers  frankly the drug dealers in my book most  of the drug dealers in the country are  high school former high school athletes  honor roll students tree trimmers  waitresses I mean most users also deal  I'm not I'm not talking about the big  dealer so I'm talking about they're  using their in their deal  to support their using so they won't be  dopesick President Trump said he was  going to declare it a national emergency  he declared it something that sounded  like a natural emergency it's a public  health emergency so it released no new  funds or authority and then the most  recent bill through Congress you know is  did all the right things just not at the  scale that is equal to the crisis right  so all the things like you know going  after fentanyl that's being mail-order  from Hong Kong that's great you know  improving the screening for drug illegal  drugs coming in from the mail and then  but there was a lot of treatment  including some more efforts to beef up  ma tea but it's just tweaking around the  edges of the issue it's not really it's  not like AIDS and HIV which finally you  know after many years because of a lot  of groundswell activism we were able to  make treatment on demand and and lots of  harm reduction  you know syringe exchange services one  of my main characters who sadly was  murdered at the end of the book somebody  ID followed for almost three years you  know she said what we need our urgent  care centers for the addicted urgent  care for the addicted she ironically had  gotten addicted initially at a local  urgent care and because she kept falling  through the gaps in these treatments she  ended up revolving in a world of  criminals and prostitution rings in  order not to get dope sick and when I  went back and I saw that first interview  in November of 2015 she's saying we need  urgent care for the indict it I thought  I mean it just gave me chills because  she was so right and what I see as  urgent care for the addicted is some  national harm reduction strategy where  somebody who's living on the streets can  go to call it a syringe exchange call it  recovery call it a treatment triage  center and they can get access to harm  reduction services like clean needles  hepatitis C HIV treatment and testing  and best of all when they're ready for  it because many people  aren't initially when they're ready for  it then they could get access to  treatment and that's just what we're not  doing a few states have like they have  they'll start ma T in the ER if you  overdose and you end up in the ER so  they have what's called hub-and-spoke  model model where they were foreal  they'll stick with you until you get to  that first appointment it's really great  in those New England states  Massachusetts Rhode Island Vermont are  the ones where we're starting to see a  slight decline in overdose deaths so we  know we know it works  all the studies say it works and now we  have like evidence in certain states  that are applying this and these are  early Medicaid expansion states you know  we still have 14 states that haven't  expanded Medicaid yet so I mean that's  another big thing that we can do and  we're still fighting Obamacare a lot of  what we've been talking about is in Beth  Macy's book dopesick dealers doctors and  the drug company that addicted America  Beth thanks so much for talking to me  about the book  Thank You David really appreciate it

